[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Syntax, Types, and Variables in Go (Golang)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Welcome to this technical tutorial on the fundamentals of Go. In this guide, we will explore the basic syntax structure, built-in data types, and how to effectively declare and manage variables."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Basic Syntax and Program Structure"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every Go program is made up of packages. The entry point of an executable program is always the main function inside the main package."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    fmt.Println("Hello, Go!")
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Variables and Declaration"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go is statically typed. Variables are explicitly declared and must be used by the compiler. You can declare variables using the 'var' keyword or the short assignment operator ':='."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    // Using var with explicit type
    var name string = "Golang"

    // Type inference using var
    var version = 1.21

    // Short assignment operator (inside functions only)
    isAwesome := true

    fmt.Println(name, version, isAwesome)
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Basic Data Types"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go provides a rich set of built-in types to handle numeric data, text, and logical values."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Booleans: 'bool' represents boolean values and can be either true or false."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Numeric Types: Integers ('int', 'int8', 'int16', 'int32', 'int64', 'uint', etc.) and Floating-point numbers ('float32', 'float64')."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Strings: 'string' represents a sequence of bytes, usually interpreted as UTF-8 encoded text."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Zero Values"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Variables declared without an explicit initial value are given their 'zero value'. This prevents uninitialized memory bugs."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"0 for numeric types"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"false for the boolean type"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":""" (the empty string) for strings"},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    var i int
    var f float64
    var b bool
    var s string

    fmt.Printf("%v %v %v %q\n", i, f, b, s)
}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"You now understand the foundational concepts of Go syntax, variable declarations, and data types. Practice writing small snippets to reinforce these concepts."}]
